WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.080
Imagine you have a tiny folder on your computer desktop, just like 488 text documents.

00:09.660 --> 00:11.280
Right. Not very big at all.

00:11.480 --> 00:16.560
Exactly. I mean, you could fit that entire folder on the absolute cheapest thumb drive you can find at a gas station,

00:16.680 --> 00:20.260
and you would still have, you know, 99% of the storage space left over.

00:20.640 --> 00:21.200
Oh, easily.

00:21.680 --> 00:26.940
But mathematically, and this is wild, locked inside that tiny, unassuming little folder

00:26.940 --> 00:31.220
are over 19 million unique essays just waiting to be written.

00:31.820 --> 00:34.540
Over 19.3 million, to be exact.

00:34.740 --> 00:35.020
Wow.

00:35.660 --> 00:38.560
And that is just if you group them into sets of three.

00:38.980 --> 00:43.300
I mean, if you start adding more files, that number explodes into the hundreds of millions.

00:43.300 --> 00:47.340
It happens faster than most human minds can even comprehend.

00:48.560 --> 00:50.860
Which brings up the ultimate modern dilemma, right?

00:51.720 --> 00:55.760
How on earth do you find the brilliant ideas hidden in those millions of possibilities

00:55.760 --> 01:00.160
without just, without completely drowning in absolute garbage?

01:00.680 --> 01:02.440
Yeah, that is the million dollar question.

01:02.900 --> 01:07.840
Because, you know, we are constantly sold this dream that managing our knowledge is just a software problem.

01:08.000 --> 01:09.620
Oh, totally. Like, just buy this app.

01:09.780 --> 01:10.000
Right.

01:10.240 --> 01:13.160
You have too many articles to read, so you get an app that saves them.

01:13.840 --> 01:19.300
You have too many notes, so you subscribe to some new tool that links them all together with pretty visual graphs.

01:19.300 --> 01:24.680
And you point, you click, and suddenly the machine tells you that you are now officially organized.

01:25.040 --> 01:26.480
Yeah, but...

01:26.480 --> 01:29.600
But the interface just gives you the illusion of control.

01:29.760 --> 01:36.660
I mean, it feels incredibly comforting to see your information categorized and filed away into neat digital buckets, you know?

01:36.820 --> 01:37.040
Sure.

01:37.620 --> 01:44.060
The problem arises when you transition from merely storing information to actually synthesizing it.

01:44.300 --> 01:47.200
When you step into the world of trying to genuinely think.

01:47.200 --> 01:48.200
Exactly.

01:48.980 --> 01:54.840
You sit down, you stare at all those bookmarks, all those highlighted paragraphs, all those saved PDFs.

01:55.260 --> 01:59.600
And you try to connect them into a genuinely new original argument.

01:59.880 --> 02:04.040
And that beautiful filing system just completely breaks down.

02:04.200 --> 02:04.820
It totally does.

02:05.520 --> 02:12.700
We're looking at a cognitive landscape that feels more like a hoarder's garage of digital text than a functional library.

02:13.020 --> 02:15.120
A hoarder's garage is a great way to put it.

02:15.120 --> 02:20.840
You save and you save, but the sheer volume of information makes actually utilizing it feel...

02:20.840 --> 02:21.720
Totally paralyzing.

02:22.040 --> 02:24.240
You end up with a massive repository, but no map.

02:24.360 --> 02:24.560
Right.

02:24.940 --> 02:31.640
And more importantly, no mechanism to force those disparate pieces of information into a meaningful conversation with one another.

02:31.900 --> 02:35.180
I mean, this is the exact definition of informational paralysis.

02:35.180 --> 02:41.640
Which is why today, we are going on a deep dive into an absolutely fascinating stack of source material.

02:42.060 --> 02:47.620
We are looking at a collection of documents written by an independent researcher who goes by the name Flyxion.

02:47.960 --> 02:49.640
Published in October of 2026?

02:49.900 --> 02:50.100
Yeah.

02:50.440 --> 02:56.140
And I have to tell you, going through these documents, this isn't just a list of productivity tips and tricks.

02:56.420 --> 02:57.240
Oh, not at all.

02:57.240 --> 03:03.020
These papers detail a custom-built, highly eccentric, AI-driven writing and thinking system.

03:03.460 --> 03:10.820
But as you dig into the architecture of what they actually built, you realize it's, well, it's a profound philosophical journey.

03:11.220 --> 03:11.740
It really is.

03:12.020 --> 03:16.680
It's an exploration of what it means to think alongside machines without surrendering your own judgment.

03:17.020 --> 03:17.260
Right.

03:17.960 --> 03:21.420
And our mission for this deep dive is to trace that exact journey.

03:21.780 --> 03:24.500
The scope of the material is what makes it so compelling, honestly.

03:24.500 --> 03:25.040
Yeah.

03:25.360 --> 03:29.100
Because Flyxion doesn't just outline a technical pipeline, right?

03:29.440 --> 03:29.820
No.

03:30.060 --> 03:36.120
They plunge into the dizzying mathematics of human creativity, map out the brutal economic realities of automation,

03:36.300 --> 03:44.200
and then, in perhaps the most radical move, attempt to use hardcore formal mathematical logic to solve a deeply human dilemma.

03:44.700 --> 03:49.480
The dilemma of whether or not to actually hit the publish button on their own work.

03:49.580 --> 03:50.020
Exactly.

03:50.020 --> 03:55.180
So we are going to explore this researcher's custom system for recombining ideas.

03:55.620 --> 03:59.540
We're going to unpack the staggering math of that corpus we mentioned.

03:59.700 --> 04:01.160
The 19 million potential ideas.

04:01.300 --> 04:01.560
Yes.

04:01.820 --> 04:12.020
We're going to dive deep into these statistical traps of relying on AI and look at that wild attempt to use formal process algebra to make an editorial decision.

04:12.660 --> 04:12.940
Okay.

04:13.160 --> 04:13.960
Let's unpack this.

04:13.960 --> 04:20.740
Well, setting the stage for what this system actually is requires us to look at the default setting for artificial intelligence today.

04:21.060 --> 04:23.120
Which is, what, mostly just chatbots.

04:23.360 --> 04:23.820
Right.

04:24.060 --> 04:27.680
Currently, the standard interaction model is incredibly frictionless.

04:27.920 --> 04:33.660
You give a language model a prompt, and it instantly spits out a highly fluent, very confident-sounding essay.

04:33.900 --> 04:36.160
Very confident, even when it's totally wrong.

04:36.160 --> 04:36.800
Exactly.

04:37.180 --> 04:42.800
It smooths over all the logical gaps, and it might be completely hallucinating its facts, but it reads beautifully.

04:43.340 --> 04:50.260
Flyxion's system, which is detailed in a document title from Corpus to Argument, fundamentally rejects that frictionless model.

04:50.460 --> 04:52.340
The system actively creates friction.

04:52.740 --> 04:53.520
It really does.

04:53.580 --> 04:55.980
It refuses to just be a standard summarizer.

04:56.380 --> 04:56.660
Right.

04:56.760 --> 04:59.640
Instead, the author built a strictly staged architecture.

04:59.640 --> 05:08.540
They broke the thinking process down into four distinct phases, classification, retrieval, recombination, and composition.

05:09.260 --> 05:13.000
And the engine driving all of this is a custom tool they call Yarnwinder.

05:13.420 --> 05:14.020
Yarnwinder.

05:14.160 --> 05:15.440
It's very descriptive.

05:16.340 --> 05:21.120
The separation of these operations is the critical security feature of the entire design.

05:21.260 --> 05:25.980
I mean, the researcher points out a massive flaw in standard AI writing tools.

05:26.320 --> 05:28.180
That they hide the hallucinations.

05:28.660 --> 05:29.020
Exactly.

05:29.020 --> 05:39.840
They produce fluent prose while totally concealing the difference between a real claim from a source document and a random conjecture introduced by the AI just to make the paragraph flow better.

05:40.040 --> 05:41.020
Right. To make it sound nice.

05:41.460 --> 05:44.720
But Flyxion's design makes that difference persistent and visible.

05:45.260 --> 05:49.020
The output of one specific operation becomes the strict input for the next.

05:49.540 --> 05:51.080
I love how they phrase it in the text.

05:51.200 --> 05:55.760
They write, um, a passage does not become reliable because it belongs to a cluster.

05:56.080 --> 05:57.240
That's a crucial point.

05:57.240 --> 05:59.160
It's such an easy trap to fall into.

05:59.300 --> 06:05.560
You see three documents grouped together under a heading, and you assume there must be a valid, truthful connection between them.

06:05.860 --> 06:06.900
Which is rarely true.

06:07.260 --> 06:10.840
So let's look at how they handle that first stage, classification.

06:10.840 --> 06:16.980
The author organizes their entire corpus of essays using what they call the four organizations.

06:17.600 --> 06:17.840
Yes.

06:18.300 --> 06:22.780
The first one is this incredibly eccentric structure called the micromegas ontology.

06:22.780 --> 06:26.860
And to understand why it's so eccentric, consider how most people tag their files.

06:27.060 --> 06:32.920
You use standard, broad categories like technology or biology or economics, right?

06:33.120 --> 06:34.200
Right. Just basic keywords.

06:34.760 --> 06:39.460
The problem with those tags is that they don't describe the behavior of the ideas inside the text.

06:39.940 --> 06:42.720
So Flyxion abandons standard tagging entirely.

06:42.720 --> 06:48.040
I was reading this list of categories and just smiling because it sounds like, I don't know, a poetic filing cabinet.

06:48.300 --> 06:48.960
It really does.

06:49.500 --> 06:53.940
Instead of biology, they use the category whales to represent living beings.

06:54.200 --> 06:58.820
They use mushrooms to represent ideas about rapid growth or sudden emergence.

06:59.320 --> 07:03.220
And trees is the category for rootedness or spatial fixation.

07:03.440 --> 07:03.920
It's wild.

07:04.620 --> 07:05.180
Way to get better.

07:05.520 --> 07:06.960
Oh, the abstraction goes much deeper.

07:07.580 --> 07:10.900
They use the term lamfrond to represent dark and baryonic matter.

07:10.900 --> 07:12.020
Lamfrond?

07:12.280 --> 07:12.420
Yeah.

07:13.080 --> 07:18.320
They use ocular xylem to stand for photonic computers combined with wood pulp building materials.

07:18.640 --> 07:20.560
Like fiber optics mixed with trees.

07:21.140 --> 07:21.500
Exactly.

07:22.200 --> 07:25.700
And lamfridine is the bucket for dark energy and expanding voids.

07:26.140 --> 07:28.180
Where did the name micromegas even come from?

07:28.480 --> 07:32.640
The name itself is a literary reference to an old philosophical story by Voltaire,

07:32.940 --> 07:36.180
though the author is very careful to keep the literary inspiration separate

07:36.180 --> 07:39.080
from the strict functional definitions of these categories.

07:39.080 --> 07:40.960
Wait, I'm trying to visualize this.

07:41.140 --> 07:41.260
Right.

07:41.260 --> 07:44.220
If I have an essay about a new fiber optic network,

07:44.700 --> 07:47.140
I don't just dump it into a folder labeled networks.

07:47.900 --> 07:50.960
I might put it in ocular xylem.

07:51.400 --> 07:51.800
Right.

07:52.100 --> 07:54.360
But it's not just based on keyword matching, is it?

07:54.380 --> 07:58.500
Like you don't throw a document into the lamfrond bucket just because the word matter

07:58.500 --> 07:59.660
appears in the third paragraph.

07:59.740 --> 08:00.520
No, not at all.

08:01.040 --> 08:04.460
The assignment depends entirely on argumentative relevance.

08:04.740 --> 08:05.880
This is a crucial distinction.

08:06.660 --> 08:06.880
Okay.

08:06.880 --> 08:08.000
What does that mean in practice?

08:08.460 --> 08:12.740
Well, the system's record actually notes how the category is being used in the text.

08:13.580 --> 08:15.880
Is lamfrond the literal subject of the paper?

08:16.260 --> 08:19.360
Is it playing an explanatory role to describe something else?

08:19.620 --> 08:22.080
Or is it merely being used as a passing analogy?

08:22.920 --> 08:23.440
Oh, wow.

08:23.960 --> 08:25.500
So it tracks the function.

08:25.920 --> 08:26.400
Exactly.

08:27.260 --> 08:30.220
By tracking the argumentative function rather than just the topic,

08:30.220 --> 08:34.140
the system preserves the actual intellectual shape of the source material.

08:34.700 --> 08:37.620
That makes standard keyword tagging look incredibly primitive.

08:37.740 --> 08:38.400
It really does.

08:38.980 --> 08:41.040
And that's just the first of the four organizations.

08:41.600 --> 08:45.200
The second is topic discovery using similarity clustering.

08:45.680 --> 08:47.440
But there's a huge disclaimer here.

08:48.080 --> 08:48.460
Right.

08:48.600 --> 08:51.400
The author notes that these clusters are strictly provisional.

08:51.740 --> 08:56.220
They are just a temporary mathematical description of the corpus at a given moment.

08:56.220 --> 09:00.840
So they aren't permanent boxes where every single text must neatly fit.

09:00.980 --> 09:01.160
No.

09:01.680 --> 09:06.520
And then the third organization is where we start moving from what the texts are to what

09:06.520 --> 09:07.300
happens to them.

09:07.460 --> 09:09.560
It's a process ontology.

09:09.980 --> 09:11.040
What happens to them?

09:11.180 --> 09:12.280
Like actions?

09:12.580 --> 09:12.860
Yes.

09:13.000 --> 09:14.560
This describes transformations.

09:15.240 --> 09:18.600
Inscription, compression, routing, verification, exclusion.

09:19.160 --> 09:22.960
Think of this as categorizing the actions described in the texts.

09:22.960 --> 09:23.560
Okay.

09:23.900 --> 09:26.200
But again, Flyxion draws a hard line here.

09:26.460 --> 09:31.240
They explicitly note that describing the compression of a digital file is very different

09:31.240 --> 09:34.860
from the argumentative act of compressing an intellectual argument.

09:35.540 --> 09:40.120
Ah, so the system doesn't confuse a physical process with a cognitive one.

09:40.180 --> 09:40.560
Exactly.

09:41.220 --> 09:43.260
Which brings us to the fourth organization.

09:43.600 --> 09:45.920
And this is where the system gets really dynamic.

09:46.580 --> 09:48.080
Task relative roles.

09:48.760 --> 09:49.660
This is the fun part.

09:49.660 --> 09:53.340
This is where the artificial intelligence actually steps onto the stage.

09:53.720 --> 09:59.600
The system utilizes five distinct AI roles that prompt a local granite language model.

09:59.980 --> 10:05.200
And it's worth noting, this model is running entirely locally on the user's own machine

10:05.200 --> 10:07.000
via a tool called OLAMA.

10:07.220 --> 10:08.900
Meaning no internet connection required.

10:09.080 --> 10:09.280
Right.

10:09.880 --> 10:13.920
That means the processing is incredibly cheap and completely private.

10:14.320 --> 10:16.940
No data is being sent to a massive corporate cloud.

10:17.160 --> 10:17.900
Which is huge.

10:17.900 --> 10:22.900
Utilizing a local model changes the economics of the system entirely, which we will see becomes

10:22.900 --> 10:24.760
incredibly important later on.

10:24.900 --> 10:25.180
Oh yeah.

10:25.240 --> 10:26.080
The economic traps.

10:26.480 --> 10:27.060
We'll get there.

10:28.200 --> 10:32.400
But focusing on these five roles, what makes them brilliant isn't just their stated objectives.

10:33.140 --> 10:37.160
It's that Flyxion programmed each role with a clearly defined failure mode.

10:37.280 --> 10:37.540
Yes.

10:37.980 --> 10:42.420
The system expects the AI to fail in specific, predictable ways.

10:42.500 --> 10:45.220
And it builds the architecture around catching those failures.

10:45.220 --> 10:51.200
Let's break those five roles down, because they act like a team of highly specialized, slightly

10:51.200 --> 10:52.220
flawed assistants.

10:52.460 --> 10:53.040
Let's do it.

10:53.700 --> 10:54.760
First you have the archivist.

10:55.320 --> 10:56.480
Its job is retrieval.

10:57.120 --> 11:01.720
It combs through the text to find provenance, alternative viewpoints, and qualifications.

11:02.220 --> 11:04.400
But it's documented failure mode.

11:05.220 --> 11:07.820
The archivist risks hoarding too much context.

11:07.820 --> 11:09.720
It just wants to save everything.

11:09.940 --> 11:10.220
Exactly.

11:10.380 --> 11:14.380
Tries to bring in everything, drowning the argument in irrelevant background noise.

11:14.780 --> 11:14.980
Right.

11:15.420 --> 11:16.920
Then you introduce the formalist.

11:17.600 --> 11:23.360
This role's job is to retrieve strict definitions, structural dependencies, and logical constraints

11:23.360 --> 11:24.060
from the texts.

11:24.580 --> 11:28.820
And the formalist's specific failure mode is falling into what the author calls correct

11:28.820 --> 11:31.000
Correct, but ungrounded abstraction.

11:31.640 --> 11:33.680
Meaning it loses touch with reality.

11:34.000 --> 11:34.460
Pretty much.

11:34.580 --> 11:39.080
It might build a perfectly logical framework that has absolutely zero connection to the

11:39.080 --> 11:41.740
real world, messy reality of the source material.

11:42.080 --> 11:43.360
Next is the synthesis.

11:44.020 --> 11:47.760
This is the one that actually proposes the argumentative connections between different

11:47.760 --> 11:48.140
sources.

11:48.640 --> 11:51.740
It says, you know, hey, idea A connects to idea B.

11:52.100 --> 11:54.360
But its failure mode is a lack of convergence.

11:54.360 --> 11:59.400
It risks spinning out endless hypothetical connections without ever landing on a single

11:59.400 --> 12:00.320
solid point.

12:00.760 --> 12:02.320
It just brainstorms into infinity.

12:02.880 --> 12:03.960
Like that one guy in every meeting.

12:04.120 --> 12:05.060
Exactly like that guy.

12:05.640 --> 12:07.920
So to counter that, you have the strategist.

12:08.240 --> 12:13.740
The strategist examines the practical conditions and real world consequences of whatever the synthesis

12:13.740 --> 12:14.400
proposed.

12:14.800 --> 12:16.740
But its risk is the exact opposite.

12:17.340 --> 12:21.760
The strategist tricks deep, nuanced understanding for an immediate, shallow effect.

12:22.100 --> 12:23.820
It just wants a quick, pragmatic result.

12:23.820 --> 12:26.780
And finally, sitting above the other four is the curator.

12:27.360 --> 12:31.920
The curator looks at all the materials gathered and proposed by the others and decides if

12:31.920 --> 12:35.280
a brief is actually mature enough to be drafted into a full essay.

12:36.260 --> 12:40.060
And the curator's failure mode is perhaps the most relatable one of all.

12:40.280 --> 12:40.700
What's that?

12:41.140 --> 12:45.560
It risks spending all of its computational effort just coordinating the other roles without

12:45.560 --> 12:47.740
ever actually producing a final product.

12:48.100 --> 12:49.640
Oh man, middle management.

12:50.220 --> 12:51.080
Essentially, yes.

12:51.640 --> 12:53.600
But wait, I have to challenge this setup.

12:53.820 --> 12:58.840
Because looking at these five roles, isn't Flyxion just totally outsourcing the active

12:58.840 --> 12:59.920
thinking to the machine?

13:00.280 --> 13:01.040
It sounds like it, right?

13:01.680 --> 13:01.880
Yeah.

13:02.000 --> 13:07.280
I mean, you've got an AI finding the text, an AI extracting the definitions, an AI proposing

13:07.280 --> 13:11.360
the connection, an AI finding the practical use, and an AI deciding if it's ready.

13:11.780 --> 13:13.100
Where's the human in this loop?

13:13.980 --> 13:16.560
That is the exact trap the author is navigating.

13:16.560 --> 13:18.940
And it's why the system is designed the way it is.

13:18.940 --> 13:25.100
What's fascinating here is that the architecture explicitly strips the AI of final authority.

13:25.640 --> 13:26.140
Wait, really?

13:26.620 --> 13:26.980
How?

13:27.740 --> 13:31.340
None of these five roles actually own a passage of text.

13:31.580 --> 13:33.320
They are merely running queries.

13:33.660 --> 13:36.760
The system runs successive, isolated prompts to the model.

13:36.760 --> 13:39.900
So they don't actually write the final paper?

13:40.320 --> 13:40.440
No.

13:40.920 --> 13:41.980
Consider how they interact.

13:42.520 --> 13:44.120
The synthesis proposes a connection.

13:44.620 --> 13:45.800
It doesn't write the essay.

13:45.920 --> 13:46.820
It just makes a proposal.

13:47.160 --> 13:47.320
Okay.

13:47.800 --> 13:52.500
Then, the formalist is called in to examine the logical premises of that specific connection.

13:53.540 --> 13:58.580
Next, the archivist is sent back into the corpus on a second retrieval pass, specifically

13:58.580 --> 14:02.140
to find evidence for or against the synthesis's proposal.

14:02.680 --> 14:03.440
Checking its work.

14:04.000 --> 14:04.340
Exactly.

14:05.120 --> 14:09.440
Then, the strategist looks for a discriminating test to prove the connection false in the

14:09.440 --> 14:09.940
real world.

14:10.520 --> 14:14.480
Every single step is just the generation of a heavily scrutinized proposal.

14:14.960 --> 14:20.380
So it's less like an automated factory and more like managing a team of very eager but

14:20.380 --> 14:22.080
completely untrustworthy employees.

14:22.600 --> 14:23.780
That's a good way to look at it.

14:24.140 --> 14:25.880
Let's imagine a bespoke analogy here.

14:25.880 --> 14:31.920
The AI is like a hyperactive librarian who can run through the stacks and fetch books

14:31.920 --> 14:34.420
instantly, but they can't actually read the books.

14:34.960 --> 14:35.060
Right.

14:35.560 --> 14:39.060
They bring you a stack and say, look, both of these books have red covers.

14:39.320 --> 14:40.720
They must be about the same thing.

14:41.580 --> 14:45.800
And then you have an overconfident debater standing next to the librarian who is willing

14:45.800 --> 14:48.600
to argue any point, regardless of whether it's true.

14:48.600 --> 14:49.940
And where do you fit in?

14:50.480 --> 14:53.340
The human author is the senior partner sitting at the desk.

14:53.880 --> 14:57.560
The team can bring the senior partner all the materials and propose all the arguments,

14:57.880 --> 15:01.460
but the senior partner is the only one who can actually sign off on the brief.

15:01.800 --> 15:03.400
That captures the dynamic perfectly.

15:03.880 --> 15:08.220
And that final sign-off happens in the core mechanism of the yarn winder composition system,

15:08.660 --> 15:10.180
the ledger of obligations.

15:10.180 --> 15:10.740
Yes.

15:11.500 --> 15:15.740
Let's talk about this ledger because it fundamentally changes how AI evaluates its own work.

15:16.240 --> 15:21.080
In previous iterations of this kind of software, people always used numerical scores, didn't

15:21.080 --> 15:21.260
they?

15:21.380 --> 15:22.720
Almost exclusively, yeah.

15:23.500 --> 15:28.300
The AI would generate a paragraph and then run a self-evaluation prompt, like, I give this

15:28.300 --> 15:32.980
paragraph an 85 out of 100 for coherence and a 90 for stylistic flow.

15:33.380 --> 15:35.260
Which is absurd when you think about it.

15:35.740 --> 15:38.120
Flyxion abandons numerical scoring entirely.

15:38.720 --> 15:39.340
They had to.

15:39.340 --> 15:44.420
They recognized that numerical scoring is completely gameable by a language model.

15:45.020 --> 15:49.200
The model will just write flowery prose to inflate its own score without actually improving

15:49.200 --> 15:50.300
the underlying logic.

15:50.920 --> 15:55.500
So instead, yarn winder requires the AI to meet recorded textual obligations.

15:56.080 --> 15:56.320
Right.

15:56.680 --> 16:01.120
When a section of an essay is drafted, there are four strict rules it has to follow.

16:01.320 --> 16:01.700
What are they?

16:02.160 --> 16:05.580
First, source interpretations must be demonstrably accurate.

16:05.580 --> 16:11.100
Second, the connecting premise between two sources must be explicitly explained, not just

16:11.100 --> 16:11.640
implied.

16:11.800 --> 16:12.180
Makes sense.

16:12.440 --> 16:17.300
Third, any material objections found in the texts must receive a direct answer or a concession.

16:17.860 --> 16:22.640
And fourth, any open claims that aren't fully proven must remain explicitly listed as open.

16:22.640 --> 16:24.540
So you can't just sweep problems under the rug.

16:24.760 --> 16:25.080
Exactly.

16:25.900 --> 16:29.260
And the ledger operates on three simple states for each of these obligations.

16:30.160 --> 16:32.280
Discharged, deferred, or open.

16:32.500 --> 16:34.040
Okay, but who updates the ledger?

16:34.500 --> 16:36.280
Well, here is the critical bottleneck.

16:36.860 --> 16:39.940
A language model might propose that an obligation is discharged.

16:40.100 --> 16:43.480
It might generate a note saying, you know, look, I answered the counter argument.

16:43.480 --> 16:44.700
Here is the citation.

16:45.060 --> 16:46.660
Change the status to discharged.

16:46.900 --> 16:47.180
But?

16:47.520 --> 16:51.440
But the system physically prevents the AI from changing that status.

16:52.060 --> 16:55.200
Only the human reader using their own judgment can confirm it.

16:55.560 --> 16:55.940
Wow.

16:56.500 --> 17:01.320
So the ledger keeps the AI's optimistic proposal strictly walled off from the human's final

17:01.320 --> 17:01.960
confirmations.

17:02.200 --> 17:02.720
Exactly.

17:03.440 --> 17:08.200
It is mathematically and structurally designed to prevent the AI from grading its own homework.

17:08.360 --> 17:08.940
That's brilliant.

17:08.940 --> 17:14.340
It ensures that a section of text might be accepted by the human as a conjectural argument,

17:14.700 --> 17:18.620
but the prose has to represent that conjectural status accurately.

17:18.920 --> 17:21.460
So if there's a flaw, it stays visible.

17:21.800 --> 17:22.020
Right.

17:22.620 --> 17:27.720
If there's a major logical objection that defeats a central claim, the AI is not allowed to just

17:27.720 --> 17:32.100
write a stylistically prettier version of the claim to bypass the objection.

17:32.660 --> 17:37.120
The claim must be formally withdrawn or explicitly restricted in the text.

17:37.120 --> 17:41.540
The human is the ultimate unbypassable bottleneck for truth.

17:42.100 --> 17:43.120
The human is the bottleneck.

17:43.260 --> 17:43.460
Mm-hmm.

17:44.140 --> 17:48.500
Which, well, that leads us directly into the most daunting aspect of this entire experiment.

17:49.020 --> 17:49.520
The math.

17:49.740 --> 17:50.060
Yes.

17:50.640 --> 17:55.420
Because if the human is the bottleneck, we have to grapple with the sheer, terrifying volume

17:55.420 --> 17:59.380
of what this hyperactive AI librarian is capable of proposing.

18:00.300 --> 18:04.680
Let's look at the second document in our stack, titled 19 Million Possible Essays.

18:04.680 --> 18:09.000
The math Flyxion presents in this section is genuinely dizzying.

18:09.140 --> 18:09.740
It really is.

18:10.420 --> 18:13.700
To test this system, the researcher took a snapshot of their personal corpus.

18:14.440 --> 18:18.240
After filtering out all the duplicates and keeping only the longest version of each text,

18:18.680 --> 18:21.800
they were left with exactly 488 eligible documents.

18:22.460 --> 18:27.840
And as we mentioned at the start, 488 documents is a minuscule amount of data in the grand scheme

18:27.840 --> 18:28.260
of things.

18:28.580 --> 18:28.780
Right.

18:28.840 --> 18:30.280
It's a tiny footprint on a hard drive.

18:30.280 --> 18:35.940
But combinatorics, the mathematics of counting and arranging sets, reveals the hidden density

18:35.940 --> 18:36.940
of that tiny folder.

18:38.020 --> 18:38.220
Okay.

18:38.240 --> 18:42.440
Let's walk through the math slowly for me and everyone else.

18:43.100 --> 18:48.740
If you want the system to select just two documents from that folder to compare, you use a simple

18:48.740 --> 18:50.080
combinatorial formula.

18:50.320 --> 18:50.540
Right.

18:50.540 --> 18:56.000
You take the number of documents, multiply it by that number minus 1, and divide by 2.

18:56.960 --> 18:59.520
The formula is like n times n minus 1 divided by 2.

19:00.660 --> 19:05.260
So 488 times 487 divided by 2.

19:05.780 --> 19:11.080
And that yields 118,828 possible unique pairs.

19:11.420 --> 19:13.140
Over 100,000 pairs.

19:13.540 --> 19:16.700
That is already a staggering number for a human to reveal.

19:16.880 --> 19:18.320
It's impossible for one person.

19:18.320 --> 19:21.160
But the yarn winder system isn't just looking at pairs.

19:21.720 --> 19:26.700
It is designed to evaluate triples, bringing three separate documents into conversation

19:26.700 --> 19:27.440
with one another.

19:27.840 --> 19:29.500
And this is where the numbers go off the charts.

19:29.980 --> 19:33.000
Because if you select three documents, the formula expands.

19:33.680 --> 19:38.660
It becomes n times n minus 1 times n minus 2 divided by 6.

19:39.300 --> 19:40.760
Do you want to explain the division?

19:41.320 --> 19:41.520
Yeah.

19:41.600 --> 19:42.920
So why divide by 6?

19:42.920 --> 19:47.860
Because in a pure mathematical combination, the order you select the documents doesn't

19:47.860 --> 19:48.120
matter.

19:48.920 --> 19:51.700
Document ABC is the same grouping as CBA.

19:52.020 --> 19:52.240
Right.

19:52.460 --> 19:56.080
There's six ways to arrange three items, so you divide by 6 to remove the duplicates.

19:56.340 --> 19:56.700
Exactly.

19:57.100 --> 20:06.400
And when you run that math for 488 documents, it yields 19,250,136 possible unique triples.

20:06.640 --> 20:07.940
Over 19 million.

20:07.940 --> 20:12.820
Combine that with the pairs, and you have over 19.3 million unique combinations.

20:13.480 --> 20:15.240
And this number grows cubically.

20:15.780 --> 20:17.040
Which means it gets huge.

20:17.360 --> 20:17.760
Fast.

20:18.380 --> 20:20.960
Flyxion points out a terrifying reality here.

20:21.560 --> 20:26.660
If you just add 12 more documents to your folder, bringing the total to an even 500, you

20:26.660 --> 20:28.000
don't just add a few more options.

20:28.280 --> 20:29.100
How many do you add?

20:29.720 --> 20:35.060
Adding those 12 files generates over 1.46 million brand new triples.

20:35.440 --> 20:36.720
From just 12 files?

20:36.720 --> 20:37.280
Yes.

20:37.820 --> 20:40.240
The combinatorial explosion is profound.

20:40.600 --> 20:40.720
Wow.

20:41.360 --> 20:47.080
But, and this is a big but, this is where Flyxion is incredibly meticulous with their terminology,

20:47.500 --> 20:48.900
and we have to be careful as well.

20:49.940 --> 20:54.640
19 million combinations absolutely do not equal 19 million essays.

20:55.180 --> 20:56.100
No, not at all.

20:56.360 --> 20:57.900
Let's break down the distinction they make.

20:58.300 --> 21:01.420
They separate the intellectual output into three distinct objects.

21:01.840 --> 21:05.200
A combination, a brief, and an essay.

21:05.200 --> 21:06.840
Okay, what is the difference?

21:07.320 --> 21:09.120
A combination is purely mathematical.

21:09.720 --> 21:13.800
It is just a set of two or three source files sitting next to each other on a hard drive.

21:14.000 --> 21:16.060
There is no argument there, just proximity.

21:16.520 --> 21:16.620
Right.

21:16.620 --> 21:22.580
A brief is that combination, but it has been assigned a specific relation by the system,

21:22.840 --> 21:26.300
and it has been given an ordering of the AI roles we discussed earlier.

21:26.680 --> 21:28.940
It is a structured prompt waiting to be executed.

21:29.360 --> 21:29.600
Okay.

21:29.800 --> 21:30.360
And the essay?

21:31.080 --> 21:33.680
An essay is the incredibly rare final product.

21:34.080 --> 21:38.700
It is a fully human-vetted, deeply developed argument that has actually survived the rigorous

21:38.700 --> 21:40.340
testing of the ledger of obligations.

21:40.340 --> 21:45.140
Most combinations will never become briefs, and the vast majority of briefs will never

21:45.140 --> 21:45.780
become essays.

21:46.280 --> 21:46.380
Right.

21:47.040 --> 21:50.780
Okay, so let's talk about those relations, because this is the mechanism that actually

21:50.780 --> 21:52.940
forces the AI to think in interesting ways.

21:53.020 --> 21:54.180
Yes, the relations are key.

21:55.100 --> 21:59.540
Because the system doesn't just hand the AI three documents and say, hey, compare these.

22:00.000 --> 22:03.020
That would result in a generic Wikipedia-style summary.

22:03.020 --> 22:03.660
Exactly.

22:04.700 --> 22:09.400
Instead, the generator randomly applies one of five specific relations to the combination.

22:10.400 --> 22:11.660
First is transfer.

22:12.460 --> 22:18.980
This tells the AI to identify a working mechanism from source A and attempt to apply it to a problem

22:18.980 --> 22:20.320
found in source B.

22:21.160 --> 22:22.260
Second is tension.

22:22.860 --> 22:26.980
This instructs the AI to find a substantive, logical tension between the sources.

22:26.980 --> 22:33.600
But, and this is important, it explicitly forbids it from manufacturing fake, superficial disagreement

22:33.600 --> 22:34.680
just for the sake of drama.

22:34.800 --> 22:35.780
Third is composition.

22:36.460 --> 22:42.100
This asks the AI if the distinct processes described in A and B can be composed together

22:42.100 --> 22:43.720
into a single, larger pipeline.

22:44.260 --> 22:45.300
Fourth is boundary.

22:45.640 --> 22:51.880
The AI must use the facts in one source to construct a boundary case or a hard limit for the theories

22:51.880 --> 22:52.760
in the other source.

22:52.760 --> 22:58.520
And the fifth relation, which is used exclusively when dealing with triples, is triangulation.

22:58.980 --> 23:01.500
Triangulation is particularly brilliant.

23:02.220 --> 23:04.160
Imagine you draw three random documents.

23:04.640 --> 23:07.720
Source A might be a paper on urban traffic patterns.

23:08.140 --> 23:11.720
Source B is a study on the fluid dynamics of water through pipes.

23:12.200 --> 23:15.560
And Source C is an essay on the psychological stress of commuting.

23:15.780 --> 23:17.480
Okay, three totally different things.

23:17.620 --> 23:17.780
Right.

23:18.120 --> 23:21.460
The triangulation relation forces the AI into a strict framework.

23:21.460 --> 23:24.080
Source A must supply the core problem.

23:24.740 --> 23:27.900
Source B must supply a potential mechanism to solve it.

23:28.580 --> 23:33.440
And Source C must act as a strict constraint or counterexample that complicates the solution.

23:34.080 --> 23:34.440
Oh, man.

23:34.860 --> 23:39.980
You can instantly see how multiplying those 19 million combinations by five different relations,

23:40.480 --> 23:43.540
and then multiplying that by the different ways you can order the sources,

23:43.540 --> 23:47.980
because using A to solve B is very different from using B to solve A.

23:48.160 --> 23:52.480
It multiplies the total number of possible briefs several times over.

23:52.800 --> 23:55.780
We are talking about tens of millions of structured prompts.

23:56.160 --> 23:56.400
Yes.

23:56.840 --> 24:00.200
But, as you mentioned, most of those briefs are going to be absolute garbage.

24:00.800 --> 24:07.360
I mean, if the random generator combines a deeply technical essay on astrophysics with a personal essay on studio pottery,

24:07.360 --> 24:10.760
there might not be a valid intellectual bridge between them at all.

24:11.420 --> 24:12.040
Usually not.

24:12.860 --> 24:17.060
But the magic of the system is that occasionally there is a valid bridge.

24:17.300 --> 24:22.500
And Flyxion provides a stunning example of this in the documents, which they call the worked case.

24:22.640 --> 24:23.200
Yes.

24:23.920 --> 24:25.480
Let's walk through the worked case.

24:26.200 --> 24:29.340
The system was running random samples and hit seed number seven.

24:29.840 --> 24:31.980
It pulled two incredibly different documents.

24:32.300 --> 24:32.900
Tell them what they were.

24:32.900 --> 24:38.160
The first was an essay focused on information security and the routing of digital ideas.

24:38.760 --> 24:43.920
The second was an essay about building ecologically integrated computational infrastructure.

24:44.760 --> 24:50.660
Specifically, finding ways to put the massive thermal heat output of computer servers to use in local ecosystems.

24:51.180 --> 24:51.440
Right.

24:52.320 --> 24:55.060
So you have a digital security verification on one hand,

24:55.420 --> 24:58.060
and thermodynamics and building management on the other.

24:58.380 --> 25:00.400
Two entirely distinct domains.

25:00.400 --> 25:04.200
And the generator assigned them the tension relation.

25:04.580 --> 25:09.320
So the AI had to find a substantive, logical tension between them.

25:09.940 --> 25:14.980
And the output it proposed was a genuinely insightful outline titled,

25:15.560 --> 25:17.560
Useful Heat is Not a Proof of Trust.

25:17.960 --> 25:18.680
It's a great title.

25:19.680 --> 25:21.300
The AI's conjecture was fascinating.

25:21.480 --> 25:25.340
It proposed that if a machine is generating heat to warm a building,

25:25.340 --> 25:30.340
while simultaneously participating in a cryptographic security verification system,

25:30.780 --> 25:32.720
like a proof-of-work blockchain,

25:33.560 --> 25:35.860
it is performing two distinct services.

25:36.160 --> 25:36.280
Right.

25:36.920 --> 25:41.220
But the success of the machine in heating the building does not actually prove

25:41.220 --> 25:43.320
that its security decisions are valid.

25:44.260 --> 25:47.140
A bad actor could easily fabricate false security submissions

25:47.140 --> 25:49.160
just to keep the machines running,

25:49.400 --> 25:51.020
so they could harvest the useful heat.

25:51.020 --> 25:54.120
It is a remarkable conceptual distinction.

25:54.120 --> 25:55.040
It really is.

25:55.140 --> 25:59.400
The AI successfully isolated the scalar usefulness of the heat output

25:59.400 --> 26:02.860
from the binary logical guarantee of the security system.

26:03.140 --> 26:03.860
So smart.

26:04.260 --> 26:05.560
And even more impressively,

26:05.920 --> 26:10.180
the generated outline anticipated the strongest objection to its own premise.

26:10.460 --> 26:13.740
It noted that while heat isn't a proof of trust,

26:14.240 --> 26:17.980
shutting off the heat might act as a penalty for a breach of trust.

26:17.980 --> 26:19.780
It's an incredible insight.

26:20.380 --> 26:23.620
But, well, it brings up a massive concern.

26:24.140 --> 26:25.580
Oh, I know where you're going with this.

26:25.840 --> 26:27.820
Here's where it gets really interesting.

26:28.340 --> 26:31.000
If this system is churning out millions of these prompts,

26:31.700 --> 26:35.560
and the AI is capable of producing insights that sound this smart,

26:36.260 --> 26:39.200
how do we know the AI isn't just hallucinating connections?

26:39.640 --> 26:40.760
It's a very real danger.

26:40.760 --> 26:44.960
I mean, it's like having a Dewey Decimal system for ideas that haven't been written yet.

26:45.460 --> 26:49.580
The danger is that the AI is just playing Mad Libs with academic jargon.

26:50.220 --> 26:54.480
It knows what a tension essay sounds like, so it just fakes one.

26:55.120 --> 26:58.660
Flyxion defines that exact risk as fluency.

26:59.260 --> 27:03.580
A highly capable language model can produce a plausible-sounding semantic bridge

27:03.580 --> 27:06.620
between literally any two texts you give it.

27:07.180 --> 27:09.620
Plausibility is computationally cheap for an AI.

27:09.620 --> 27:11.620
Plausibility is cheap.

27:12.380 --> 27:13.480
Truth is expensive.

27:14.300 --> 27:14.720
Exactly.

27:15.780 --> 27:17.900
The system attempts to defend against this fluency

27:17.900 --> 27:21.240
by injecting strict negative constraints into the prompts.

27:21.820 --> 27:23.880
It explicitly instructs the AI that

27:23.880 --> 27:27.140
shared words do not establish shared mechanisms.

27:27.980 --> 27:28.300
Ah.

27:28.860 --> 27:32.040
So, just because both essays use the word network

27:32.040 --> 27:33.780
doesn't mean they are talking about the same thing.

27:33.980 --> 27:34.260
Right.

27:34.260 --> 27:38.120
It also explicitly forbids the AI from inventing quotations,

27:38.120 --> 27:41.760
fabricating survey results, or hallucinating data to make the bridge work.

27:41.980 --> 27:43.960
But do those constraints actually hold?

27:44.380 --> 27:46.700
Ultimately, those prompt constraints are fragile.

27:47.040 --> 27:49.580
The only real robust defense is the human reader

27:49.580 --> 27:52.840
who has to sit down, look at the AI's proposed bridge,

27:52.880 --> 27:55.440
and check it against the full original source texts

27:55.440 --> 27:57.360
to ensure the logic actually holds.

27:57.360 --> 28:01.080
And to manage this overwhelming ocean of ideas,

28:01.220 --> 28:03.180
the author did something truly wild.

28:03.720 --> 28:09.040
They built a mathematical map of the entire 19 million combination space.

28:09.280 --> 28:12.000
Using something called the combinatorial number system.

28:12.500 --> 28:13.820
Let's unpack how this works,

28:14.200 --> 28:16.620
because it solves a massive database problem.

28:17.000 --> 28:20.140
If you try to generate and save 19 million individual text files,

28:20.400 --> 28:22.300
your hard drive would be a nightmare to navigate.

28:22.360 --> 28:23.040
It would be unusable.

28:23.040 --> 28:26.280
So instead, the author used a mathematical theorem

28:26.280 --> 28:30.120
to assign a single permanent integer to every possible triple.

28:30.920 --> 28:32.860
They built an append-only registry.

28:33.520 --> 28:35.960
You could walk up to the system and type in an integer,

28:36.280 --> 28:39.420
say, combination number 14,201,

28:39.680 --> 28:41.680
and the system instantly uses the math

28:41.680 --> 28:45.480
to regenerate that exact, specific grouping of three documents.

28:45.680 --> 28:49.540
It creates a permanent numerical address system for unwritten thoughts.

28:49.860 --> 28:52.320
Which brings us to the perfect literary comparison.

28:53.160 --> 28:56.000
Flyxion explicitly compares this combinatorial space

28:56.000 --> 28:59.200
to Jorge Luis Borges' famous short story,

28:59.720 --> 29:00.700
The Library of Babel.

29:00.840 --> 29:01.160
Yes.

29:01.700 --> 29:02.920
For anyone who hasn't read it,

29:03.020 --> 29:06.840
the story describes a universe that is an infinite library.

29:07.040 --> 29:08.140
An infinite library.

29:08.340 --> 29:11.300
The library contains every single possible book

29:11.300 --> 29:14.220
that could ever be written using the basic alphabet.

29:14.800 --> 29:17.320
Because it contains every possible combination of letters,

29:17.320 --> 29:20.260
it contains the true story of your life,

29:20.580 --> 29:21.960
the cure for every disease,

29:22.340 --> 29:23.960
and the answer to every mystery.

29:24.520 --> 29:25.040
But...

29:25.040 --> 29:27.220
But, because it contains everything,

29:27.500 --> 29:29.600
the vast, overwhelming majority of the books

29:29.600 --> 29:31.380
are total random gibberish.

29:32.020 --> 29:34.900
Finding anything true or useful is mathematically impossible.

29:35.480 --> 29:38.100
The library is completely useless without a search algorithm.

29:38.100 --> 29:43.380
Flyxion makes the profound point that a combinatorial corpus of 488 documents

29:43.380 --> 29:46.380
is basically a miniature library of Babel.

29:47.060 --> 29:49.900
Yes, it's vastly smaller than Borges' infinite library,

29:50.120 --> 29:53.660
and yes, it's built from texts that already possess internal meaning

29:53.660 --> 29:55.380
because a human authored them.

29:55.380 --> 29:58.220
But the fundamental property of the library survives.

29:58.560 --> 30:00.900
Most of the combination space is pure noise

30:00.900 --> 30:03.040
relative to any specific human interest.

30:03.760 --> 30:06.440
Generating the 90 million combinations is the easy part.

30:06.840 --> 30:09.400
Building the mathematical map is the easy part.

30:09.540 --> 30:09.720
Right.

30:10.060 --> 30:12.740
A procedure that maps the space of possible ideas

30:12.740 --> 30:16.140
without providing a rigorous procedure for searching and validating them

30:16.140 --> 30:18.240
has only done the easy half of the work.

30:18.640 --> 30:20.940
The AI generator isn't creating truth.

30:20.940 --> 30:23.940
It's merely mapping the space of possible juxtapositions.

30:23.940 --> 30:26.420
And because exhaustive enumeration,

30:26.840 --> 30:31.060
meaning having the AI actually write out a brief for all 19 million combinations,

30:31.620 --> 30:36.540
would take over 36 years of continuous non-stop machine processing time.

30:36.820 --> 30:39.220
The design relies entirely on random sampling.

30:39.560 --> 30:41.200
You draw a manageable number of seeds,

30:41.440 --> 30:43.360
evaluate them, and see what the yield is.

30:43.940 --> 30:45.880
But that sheer volume of generation,

30:46.340 --> 30:50.380
combined with the AI's dangerous ability to fake fluency,

30:50.380 --> 30:54.840
forces us to look at the massive, systemic traps the author discovered.

30:55.520 --> 30:58.760
Because building a machine that can think a million times faster than you

30:58.760 --> 31:00.720
doesn't actually solve your problems.

31:01.380 --> 31:04.740
It creates entirely new, arguably more dangerous ones.

31:05.120 --> 31:07.800
Which brings us to the third major section of our deep dive,

31:08.280 --> 31:10.880
the economic and statistical traps of automation.

31:11.540 --> 31:13.060
Flyxion didn't just build this system.

31:13.140 --> 31:16.060
They subjected it to intense theoretical stress testing

31:16.060 --> 31:17.520
to see where it would break.

31:18.020 --> 31:19.520
Let's get into the document titled,

31:19.780 --> 31:23.000
The Publication Decision in the Calculus of Constructions.

31:23.380 --> 31:26.180
Because this is where the author goes deep into the economic theories

31:26.180 --> 31:30.260
of why making things more efficient actually makes our lives harder.

31:30.380 --> 31:32.500
It's so counterintuitive, but so true.

31:32.840 --> 31:35.380
The first trap they outline is the rebound effect,

31:35.960 --> 31:38.380
specifically known as the Jevons Paradox.

31:39.040 --> 31:41.500
Walk us through the history of William Stanley Jevons,

31:41.500 --> 31:44.560
because his observation about coal in the 19th century

31:44.560 --> 31:47.560
perfectly predicts our current crisis with AI.

31:48.160 --> 31:51.020
So William Stanley Jevons was an English economist

31:51.020 --> 31:53.100
working during the Industrial Revolution.

31:53.960 --> 31:58.340
In 1865, he observed a highly counterintuitive phenomenon

31:58.340 --> 31:59.800
regarding coal consumption.

32:00.540 --> 32:03.100
At the time, engineers were introducing new,

32:03.280 --> 32:04.620
highly efficient steam engines

32:04.620 --> 32:06.900
that required significantly less coal

32:06.900 --> 32:08.060
to do the same amount of work.

32:08.060 --> 32:10.500
The logical assumption was that

32:10.500 --> 32:12.120
because the engines were more efficient,

32:12.520 --> 32:14.920
the total consumption of coal in the country would decrease.

32:15.200 --> 32:15.520
Makes sense.

32:15.880 --> 32:17.120
Better engines, less coal needed.

32:17.540 --> 32:20.000
But Jevons observed the exact opposite.

32:20.700 --> 32:21.880
Because coal was now cheaper

32:21.880 --> 32:23.400
and far more efficiently utilized,

32:23.660 --> 32:26.140
it became highly profitable to use steam engines

32:26.140 --> 32:28.560
in vastly more industries and applications.

32:28.960 --> 32:29.020
Oh.

32:29.300 --> 32:33.060
The increase in efficiency actually expanded the overall demand.

32:33.560 --> 32:35.420
The more efficient the technology became,

32:35.420 --> 32:37.780
the more total coal was consumed.

32:38.060 --> 32:41.800
So if we pull that 19th century economic theory forward

32:41.800 --> 32:45.320
and apply it to our hyperactive AI research system,

32:45.320 --> 32:47.100
you see the exact same paradox.

32:47.740 --> 32:48.920
If you build an AI system

32:48.920 --> 32:51.900
that makes generating a complex intellectual brief

32:51.900 --> 32:53.620
incredibly cheap and fast,

32:53.760 --> 32:56.700
you do not actually save the human author any time.

32:56.880 --> 32:58.720
You just generate more briefs.

32:58.880 --> 33:01.200
Think about the price of a brief.

33:01.200 --> 33:05.400
The total cost is the computational cost for the AI to generate it,

33:05.800 --> 33:08.740
plus the cognitive cost for the human to review it.

33:09.360 --> 33:09.680
Exactly.

33:10.480 --> 33:13.700
The local language model drops the generation cost to near zero.

33:14.160 --> 33:15.500
But the review cost,

33:15.860 --> 33:17.060
the human sitting down,

33:17.480 --> 33:18.660
deeply reading the sources,

33:19.120 --> 33:20.200
checking the logical bridges,

33:20.440 --> 33:21.440
and confirming the ledger,

33:21.800 --> 33:23.600
that cost does not drop at all.

33:24.240 --> 33:25.300
Human attention is finite.

33:26.000 --> 33:27.880
It's the classic highway expansion problem.

33:28.380 --> 33:29.620
A city has terrible traffic,

33:29.860 --> 33:32.520
so they add three more lanes to the highway to solve the congestion.

33:32.820 --> 33:33.840
Oh yeah, induced demand.

33:34.300 --> 33:34.620
Right.

33:34.940 --> 33:36.220
Because the highway is wider,

33:36.620 --> 33:38.880
suddenly everyone in the suburbs decides to drive

33:38.880 --> 33:39.920
instead of taking the train.

33:40.400 --> 33:41.180
And within a year,

33:41.300 --> 33:43.520
the traffic is worse than it was before you built the lanes.

33:44.040 --> 33:46.100
If an author has a fixed cognitive capacity,

33:46.100 --> 33:48.300
say you can only deeply read,

33:48.520 --> 33:48.920
evaluate,

33:49.180 --> 33:51.860
and verify five complex briefs a day.

33:52.500 --> 33:54.560
But your new AI system can now generate

33:54.560 --> 33:57.160
500 highly plausible briefs a day.

33:58.020 --> 34:00.000
The improved generator doesn't make you more productive,

34:00.140 --> 34:03.000
it just creates an infinite crushing backlog.

34:04.040 --> 34:05.380
Flyxion's math proves that

34:05.380 --> 34:07.980
if the human is operating at their maximum capacity,

34:08.500 --> 34:10.780
any improvement in AI generation efficiency

34:10.780 --> 34:13.720
leads to a backlog that grows linearly and without bound.

34:13.720 --> 34:16.420
You are literally buried alive by your own efficiency.

34:17.520 --> 34:19.780
This reveals a fundamental law of automation, right?

34:19.880 --> 34:21.740
The resource that is not automated

34:21.740 --> 34:24.500
immediately becomes the ultimate bottleneck.

34:25.140 --> 34:25.460
Absolutely.

34:26.100 --> 34:28.700
And because the review burden rises so dramatically,

34:29.020 --> 34:30.880
the human starts looking for a way out.

34:31.040 --> 34:32.240
They start looking for shortcuts.

34:32.940 --> 34:35.160
They want a metric, a score, a proxy

34:35.160 --> 34:38.240
to tell them which 5% of those 500 briefs

34:38.240 --> 34:39.400
are actually worth their time.

34:40.040 --> 34:41.900
And that desperate search for a shortcut

34:41.900 --> 34:44.460
leads directly into the second massive trap,

34:45.280 --> 34:45.840
Goodhart's Law.

34:46.560 --> 34:47.880
When a measure becomes a target,

34:48.180 --> 34:49.640
it ceases to be a good measure.

34:50.100 --> 34:50.600
Precisely.

34:50.940 --> 34:53.060
The documents spend a significant amount of time

34:53.060 --> 34:56.060
exploring how numerical AI scoring metrics

34:56.060 --> 34:58.580
are inevitably gamed by the system.

34:59.300 --> 35:00.920
Flyxion categorizes this failure

35:00.920 --> 35:03.400
into three distinct types of statistical gaming,

35:04.140 --> 35:06.660
extremal, regressional, and adversarial.

35:06.660 --> 35:09.360
Let's do a deep dive into extremal gaming

35:09.360 --> 35:11.540
because the example Flyxion provides

35:11.540 --> 35:13.240
is both mathematically fascinating

35:13.240 --> 35:14.780
and deeply disturbing.

35:15.420 --> 35:16.300
Okay, lay it on me.

35:16.780 --> 35:18.240
In an earlier version of their code,

35:18.420 --> 35:20.220
before they invented the ledger of obligations,

35:20.900 --> 35:22.680
the author tested a scoring metric

35:22.680 --> 35:26.080
to evaluate the quality of the AI's generated text.

35:26.580 --> 35:28.440
They used a concept from information theory

35:28.440 --> 35:30.400
called unigram Shannon entropy.

35:31.140 --> 35:33.420
Now, unigram just means looking at text

35:33.420 --> 35:34.880
one word at a time, right?

35:35.280 --> 35:35.660
Exactly.

35:35.900 --> 35:36.100
Okay.

35:36.680 --> 35:40.180
And entropy, well, usually when we hear entropy,

35:40.320 --> 35:42.360
we think of chaos or disorder in physics.

35:42.760 --> 35:44.480
But in information theory,

35:44.940 --> 35:48.440
entropy is used to measure unpredictability or surprise.

35:48.760 --> 35:51.760
So the theory behind using entropy as a metric

35:51.760 --> 35:52.940
was quite intuitive.

35:53.580 --> 35:55.220
A good, well-written essay

35:55.220 --> 35:57.000
shouldn't be terribly repetitive.

35:57.500 --> 36:00.320
It should utilize a rich, varied vocabulary

36:00.320 --> 36:01.800
to express complex ideas.

36:01.800 --> 36:02.240
Yes.

36:02.240 --> 36:05.320
Therefore, if a text has high entropy,

36:05.740 --> 36:06.940
meaning the words are varied,

36:07.200 --> 36:08.960
unpredictable, and information-dense,

36:09.180 --> 36:11.120
it should theoretically indicate

36:11.120 --> 36:12.700
a higher quality piece of writing.

36:13.200 --> 36:14.640
The system was trying to mathematically

36:14.640 --> 36:15.960
penalize repetition.

36:16.640 --> 36:18.740
It makes total intuitive sense.

36:19.100 --> 36:20.840
If I use a diverse vocabulary,

36:21.120 --> 36:22.740
I'm probably writing a better paper

36:22.740 --> 36:25.400
than if I just use a handful of basic words.

36:25.920 --> 36:26.980
But to measure this,

36:26.980 --> 36:29.020
the system used a unit called a NAT.

36:29.760 --> 36:30.420
For context,

36:30.600 --> 36:33.160
a NAT is similar to a bit in computing.

36:33.520 --> 36:37.000
But while a bit is based on base two mathematics,

36:37.320 --> 36:38.220
zeros and ones,

36:38.940 --> 36:41.320
a NAT is based on natural logarithms.

36:41.720 --> 36:43.980
It's a way to measure the density of information.

36:44.700 --> 36:46.380
So Flyxion ran a test

36:46.380 --> 36:47.880
to see how this entropy metric

36:47.880 --> 36:50.000
actually behaved in the real world.

36:50.360 --> 36:52.060
They took a completely coherent,

36:52.440 --> 36:54.820
thoughtful, well-structured 13-word sentence.

36:54.820 --> 36:58.080
Then, they took those exact same 13 words

36:58.080 --> 36:59.740
and randomly shuffled them

36:59.740 --> 37:00.880
into absolute word salad.

37:01.200 --> 37:01.840
Complete gibberish.

37:02.780 --> 37:04.160
And what did the metric score them?

37:04.840 --> 37:06.100
Both the brilliant sentence

37:06.100 --> 37:07.300
and the absolute gibberish

37:07.300 --> 37:10.480
scored exactly 1.925 NATs of entropy.

37:11.180 --> 37:13.340
The metric was completely blind to word order.

37:13.580 --> 37:13.840
Wow.

37:14.180 --> 37:15.060
It could not distinguish

37:15.060 --> 37:16.640
a profound human thought

37:16.640 --> 37:18.440
from a random jumble of letters

37:18.440 --> 37:20.660
because the statistical diversity

37:20.660 --> 37:22.060
of the words was identical.

37:22.060 --> 37:24.080
But the extremal gaming aspect

37:24.080 --> 37:25.700
gets so much worse.

37:26.240 --> 37:27.580
Because of how the scoring formula

37:27.580 --> 37:28.300
was constructed,

37:28.720 --> 37:30.360
the system took the entropy score

37:30.360 --> 37:31.800
and subtracted it from 100

37:31.800 --> 37:33.300
to create a final grade.

37:33.620 --> 37:35.740
It actually mathematically reversed

37:35.740 --> 37:36.860
the intended penalty.

37:37.320 --> 37:37.960
Wait, what do you mean?

37:38.220 --> 37:39.360
The mathematics of the formula

37:39.360 --> 37:41.120
created an inverse relationship.

37:41.660 --> 37:43.060
A lower entropy score

37:43.060 --> 37:45.200
actually yielded a higher final grade

37:45.200 --> 37:45.800
for the AI.

37:46.140 --> 37:46.760
Oh no.

37:46.760 --> 37:48.760
And you have to ask yourself,

37:48.980 --> 37:50.780
what text has the absolute

37:50.780 --> 37:53.060
lowest possible entropy in existence?

37:53.580 --> 37:55.680
A text that is just the exact same word

37:55.680 --> 37:57.240
repeated over and over forever.

37:57.660 --> 37:58.020
Yes.

37:58.520 --> 38:00.940
The metric that was specifically built

38:00.940 --> 38:02.400
to penalize repetition

38:02.400 --> 38:05.160
literally mathematically rewarded

38:05.160 --> 38:07.480
a single token repeated infinitely.

38:07.880 --> 38:08.580
That's hilarious.

38:09.060 --> 38:09.780
And terrifying.

38:10.100 --> 38:11.500
It's the ultimate failure

38:11.500 --> 38:12.600
of extremal gaming.

38:12.920 --> 38:14.200
If the AI is programmed

38:14.200 --> 38:16.240
to maximize that final score,

38:16.940 --> 38:18.660
it won't write a nuanced essay.

38:18.880 --> 38:20.420
It will just scream the word

38:20.420 --> 38:22.320
the at you a thousand times

38:22.320 --> 38:23.440
and declare that it has

38:23.440 --> 38:24.080
written a masterpiece.

38:24.660 --> 38:25.680
Which is exactly why

38:25.680 --> 38:26.500
Flyxion abandoned

38:26.500 --> 38:27.480
mathematical scoring.

38:27.900 --> 38:29.680
But they pushed the analysis further.

38:29.800 --> 38:31.000
Because even if you avoid

38:31.000 --> 38:32.440
that specific math error

38:32.440 --> 38:34.740
and you use a highly sophisticated

38:34.740 --> 38:36.780
multi-billion parameter

38:36.780 --> 38:38.720
language model to give a subjective score

38:38.720 --> 38:39.700
from one to a hundred,

38:40.300 --> 38:42.420
you run headfirst into the second trap.

38:43.060 --> 38:43.900
Regressional gaming.

38:44.200 --> 38:44.720
Yes.

38:45.500 --> 38:46.340
This section features

38:46.340 --> 38:48.200
a brilliant statistical proof.

38:48.660 --> 38:50.480
Flyxion asks us to imagine

38:50.480 --> 38:52.000
that an AI's proxy score

38:52.000 --> 38:53.920
is made up of two distinct components.

38:54.360 --> 38:56.080
The true quality of the brief

38:56.080 --> 38:58.000
plus a random noise component.

38:58.240 --> 38:59.040
The noise represents

38:59.040 --> 39:00.440
the AI's measurement error, right?

39:00.540 --> 39:01.440
Like maybe the AI

39:01.440 --> 39:02.540
was just feeling generous

39:02.540 --> 39:03.840
or it heavily favored

39:03.840 --> 39:05.260
a specific stylistic keyword

39:05.260 --> 39:06.780
that artificially bumped the score.

39:07.080 --> 39:07.520
Exactly.

39:07.840 --> 39:09.000
True quality plus noise

39:09.000 --> 39:10.080
equals the proxy score.

39:10.440 --> 39:11.660
Now imagine you have the AI

39:11.660 --> 39:13.240
generate one thousand essays.

39:13.240 --> 39:15.160
If you sort those one thousand essays

39:15.160 --> 39:16.360
by that proxy score

39:16.360 --> 39:18.100
and select only the top ten,

39:18.740 --> 39:20.600
the mathematics definitively prove

39:20.600 --> 39:22.560
that you are inevitably selecting

39:22.560 --> 39:23.420
the essays that have

39:23.420 --> 39:25.060
the highest noise component.

39:25.520 --> 39:26.060
Wait, really?

39:26.320 --> 39:26.680
Yes.

39:27.220 --> 39:28.820
The expected value of the noise

39:28.820 --> 39:30.120
in your top selected group

39:30.120 --> 39:31.100
is strictly positive.

39:31.620 --> 39:32.440
You aren't just selecting

39:32.440 --> 39:33.220
the best essays,

39:33.460 --> 39:35.040
you are systematically selecting

39:35.040 --> 39:35.600
the essays

39:35.600 --> 39:37.840
where the AI's internal measurement error

39:37.840 --> 39:39.620
was the most massively inflated.

39:39.620 --> 39:40.220
Wow.

39:40.840 --> 39:41.880
So the AI is basically

39:41.880 --> 39:43.040
tricking you with statistics.

39:43.500 --> 39:44.340
You think you're skimming

39:44.340 --> 39:45.200
the absolute genius

39:45.200 --> 39:46.140
off the top of the pile,

39:46.300 --> 39:47.560
but you're actually just skimming

39:47.560 --> 39:48.620
the absolute maximum

39:48.620 --> 39:49.640
statistical error.

39:50.440 --> 39:51.380
Flyxion calls this

39:51.380 --> 39:53.260
the best of N illusion.

39:53.820 --> 39:55.140
Let me try an analogy here.

39:55.640 --> 39:57.060
Imagine you are rolling

39:57.060 --> 39:58.040
a 20-sided die.

39:58.840 --> 40:00.040
Because AI generation

40:00.040 --> 40:01.500
is so incredibly cheap,

40:01.860 --> 40:02.840
instead of rolling the die

40:02.840 --> 40:03.520
ten times

40:03.520 --> 40:04.560
and keeping the highest number,

40:04.860 --> 40:05.780
you roll the die

40:05.780 --> 40:06.780
a thousand times

40:06.780 --> 40:07.800
and keep the highest number.

40:07.800 --> 40:09.780
The highest score

40:09.780 --> 40:10.640
in the thousand rolls

40:10.640 --> 40:12.480
is obviously going to be higher

40:12.480 --> 40:13.640
than the highest score

40:13.640 --> 40:14.240
in the ten rolls.

40:14.700 --> 40:15.920
So you look at the results

40:15.920 --> 40:16.380
and you think

40:16.380 --> 40:17.380
the underlying system

40:17.380 --> 40:18.120
is getting better

40:18.120 --> 40:20.200
or the AI is getting smarter.

40:20.500 --> 40:22.360
But the true underlying quality,

40:22.520 --> 40:23.540
the 20-sided die,

40:24.260 --> 40:25.280
hasn't changed at all.

40:25.760 --> 40:26.840
You just roll the dice

40:26.840 --> 40:27.600
more times

40:27.600 --> 40:28.180
and select it

40:28.180 --> 40:28.840
for the absolute

40:28.840 --> 40:29.920
maximum variance.

40:30.840 --> 40:32.000
It is a devastating critique

40:32.000 --> 40:33.360
of how a vast majority

40:33.360 --> 40:34.660
of AI evaluations

40:34.660 --> 40:35.500
and leaderboards

40:35.500 --> 40:36.580
are structured today.

40:36.580 --> 40:37.940
If you select

40:37.940 --> 40:39.820
based on a noisy proxy metric,

40:39.960 --> 40:40.440
your selection

40:40.440 --> 40:41.280
will fundamentally

40:41.280 --> 40:41.900
overstate

40:41.900 --> 40:43.340
the true target value.

40:44.240 --> 40:44.880
And the third trap,

40:45.220 --> 40:46.100
adversarial gaming,

40:46.660 --> 40:47.660
brings us full circle

40:47.660 --> 40:48.540
back to the ledger

40:48.540 --> 40:49.260
of obligations

40:49.260 --> 40:50.220
we discussed earlier.

40:50.700 --> 40:51.780
If you design

40:51.780 --> 40:53.460
a beautiful ledger system,

40:53.720 --> 40:55.160
but you let the AI market's

40:55.160 --> 40:56.080
own obligations

40:56.080 --> 40:56.580
as confirmed,

40:57.720 --> 40:59.100
the AI will immediately

40:59.100 --> 40:59.580
figure out

40:59.580 --> 41:00.600
the cheapest route possible

41:00.600 --> 41:01.460
to close the ledger.

41:01.460 --> 41:02.880
It will assert

41:02.880 --> 41:03.840
that every objection

41:03.840 --> 41:05.080
has been answered perfectly

41:05.080 --> 41:06.520
without ever actually

41:06.520 --> 41:07.880
doing the intellectual work.

41:08.420 --> 41:10.100
So if numerical scores

41:10.100 --> 41:11.700
are mathematically doomed

41:11.700 --> 41:12.180
to fail

41:12.180 --> 41:12.780
because of

41:12.780 --> 41:14.740
extremal and regressional gaming

41:14.740 --> 41:17.220
and making the AI generation

41:17.220 --> 41:18.840
faster just buries you

41:18.840 --> 41:19.480
in an infinite

41:19.480 --> 41:21.100
Jevons paradox backlog,

41:22.100 --> 41:23.300
how on earth

41:23.300 --> 41:24.080
is a human supposed

41:24.080 --> 41:25.620
to survive this system?

41:25.800 --> 41:26.520
It sounds like

41:26.520 --> 41:28.000
building an automated

41:28.000 --> 41:28.820
thought machine

41:28.820 --> 41:30.200
isn't a productivity hack,

41:30.200 --> 41:30.860
it's a recipe

41:30.860 --> 41:32.440
for cognitive suicide.

41:33.380 --> 41:33.860
If we connect

41:33.860 --> 41:34.500
all these failures

41:34.500 --> 41:35.760
back to the core architecture,

41:36.160 --> 41:37.260
this is precisely

41:37.260 --> 41:38.540
why Flyxion moved

41:38.540 --> 41:39.240
to the strict,

41:39.800 --> 41:40.540
human-controlled

41:40.540 --> 41:41.900
ledger of obligations.

41:42.960 --> 41:43.680
The math proves

41:43.680 --> 41:44.680
that a metric counting

41:44.680 --> 41:46.440
in AI's self-assertions

41:46.440 --> 41:47.380
is fundamentally

41:47.380 --> 41:48.920
mathematically meaningless.

41:49.560 --> 41:50.800
So you have to step in.

41:51.300 --> 41:51.800
The only way

41:51.800 --> 41:52.860
to break the vicious cycle

41:52.860 --> 41:53.980
of the Jevons paradox

41:53.980 --> 41:54.840
in Goodhart's law

41:54.840 --> 41:55.660
is to introduce

41:55.660 --> 41:56.500
a strict,

41:56.960 --> 41:58.280
ungameable bottleneck.

41:58.780 --> 41:59.460
A human must

41:59.460 --> 42:00.740
physically break

42:00.740 --> 42:01.180
the loop.

42:02.240 --> 42:02.860
The human reader

42:02.860 --> 42:03.400
has to look

42:03.400 --> 42:03.940
at the AI's

42:03.940 --> 42:04.740
proposed connection,

42:05.180 --> 42:05.740
read the original

42:05.740 --> 42:06.500
source document,

42:06.780 --> 42:07.840
and manually,

42:08.180 --> 42:09.080
subjectively confirm

42:09.080 --> 42:09.520
the ledger.

42:09.960 --> 42:11.180
Which doesn't scale

42:11.180 --> 42:11.540
at all.

42:11.700 --> 42:12.500
It does not scale.

42:12.720 --> 42:13.440
It requires

42:13.440 --> 42:14.940
immense human effort.

42:15.600 --> 42:16.060
And that is

42:16.060 --> 42:16.820
exactly the point.

42:17.080 --> 42:17.900
The lack of scale

42:17.900 --> 42:18.720
is the primary

42:18.720 --> 42:19.480
security feature

42:19.480 --> 42:20.440
of the entire system.

42:20.700 --> 42:22.400
That is profound.

42:23.120 --> 42:24.220
The lack of scale

42:24.220 --> 42:25.740
is the security feature.

42:25.740 --> 42:28.020
We live in a tech culture

42:28.020 --> 42:29.060
that is so entirely

42:29.060 --> 42:29.960
obsessed with making

42:29.960 --> 42:31.360
every single process

42:31.360 --> 42:33.020
scale infinitely.

42:33.660 --> 42:34.960
But human judgment,

42:35.460 --> 42:36.200
true synthesis,

42:36.500 --> 42:37.040
and intellectual

42:37.040 --> 42:37.800
responsibility

42:37.800 --> 42:39.560
physically cannot scale.

42:39.740 --> 42:40.180
They can't.

42:40.580 --> 42:41.980
And that immense tension,

42:42.280 --> 42:43.140
the friction between

42:43.140 --> 42:44.960
the infinite generation

42:44.960 --> 42:46.280
capabilities of the machine

42:46.280 --> 42:47.700
and the finite,

42:48.080 --> 42:48.800
fragile judgment

42:48.800 --> 42:49.440
of the human,

42:50.100 --> 42:51.000
is what drove the author

42:51.000 --> 42:52.000
to the most eccentric

42:52.000 --> 42:54.000
and honestly mind-blowing

42:54.000 --> 42:55.080
part of this entire

42:55.080 --> 42:55.900
source material.

42:56.460 --> 42:57.020
You are referring

42:57.020 --> 42:58.380
to the formal

42:58.380 --> 42:59.220
mathematical debate

42:59.220 --> 42:59.880
in the calculus

42:59.880 --> 43:00.660
of constructions?

43:00.900 --> 43:01.220
Yes.

43:01.960 --> 43:02.500
Section 4,

43:03.040 --> 43:04.260
the calculus of decisions.

43:04.660 --> 43:05.420
Let's set the scene

43:05.420 --> 43:05.860
for the listener.

43:06.020 --> 43:06.200
Okay.

43:06.880 --> 43:07.540
The researcher,

43:07.860 --> 43:08.280
Flyxion,

43:08.500 --> 43:09.160
has built this

43:09.160 --> 43:10.540
incredible software pipeline.

43:11.000 --> 43:11.920
They have mapped out

43:11.920 --> 43:12.480
the staggering

43:12.480 --> 43:13.460
mathematical proofs

43:13.460 --> 43:14.560
about combinatorics.

43:14.760 --> 43:15.660
They have rigorously

43:15.660 --> 43:16.700
documented the economic

43:16.700 --> 43:17.640
traps of automation.

43:18.080 --> 43:18.540
They have written

43:18.540 --> 43:19.640
these two dense,

43:20.040 --> 43:20.820
brilliant essays

43:20.820 --> 43:22.160
describing this entire

43:22.160 --> 43:23.400
untested design.

43:23.620 --> 43:24.280
But they hit a wall.

43:24.520 --> 43:25.220
A huge wall.

43:25.740 --> 43:27.260
They are entirely paralyzed

43:27.260 --> 43:28.040
about whether or not

43:28.040 --> 43:28.900
to actually publish

43:28.900 --> 43:29.880
the essays on the internet

43:29.880 --> 43:31.000
because the code

43:31.000 --> 43:32.060
hasn't been fully

43:32.060 --> 43:32.680
stress-tested

43:32.680 --> 43:33.980
against a large-scale model

43:33.980 --> 43:35.080
and they are terrified

43:35.080 --> 43:36.840
that a title like

43:36.840 --> 43:38.520
19 million possible essays

43:38.520 --> 43:39.480
might be wildly

43:39.480 --> 43:40.980
misinterpreted as clickbait

43:40.980 --> 43:42.440
by people who refuse

43:42.440 --> 43:43.540
to read the underlying math.

43:43.740 --> 43:44.780
Which is a valid fear.

43:45.060 --> 43:45.340
Totally.

43:45.920 --> 43:46.640
So what do they do?

43:47.240 --> 43:48.060
Most people would just

43:48.060 --> 43:49.140
make a pros and cons list

43:49.140 --> 43:50.220
or maybe ask a colleague

43:50.220 --> 43:50.820
for advice.

43:51.500 --> 43:52.020
Not Flyxion.

43:52.740 --> 43:54.040
Flyxion writes a formal

43:54.040 --> 43:54.760
debate essay

43:54.760 --> 43:55.840
and then attempts

43:55.840 --> 43:56.740
to solve their own

43:56.740 --> 43:57.760
psychological dilemma

43:57.760 --> 43:59.020
using literal,

43:59.420 --> 44:00.720
hardcore formal mathematics.

44:01.280 --> 44:03.000
It is a breathtaking exercise

44:03.000 --> 44:04.040
in the formalization

44:04.040 --> 44:05.220
of human anxiety.

44:05.680 --> 44:06.840
Flyxion explicitly states

44:06.840 --> 44:07.420
the hypothesis

44:07.420 --> 44:08.420
they are trying to solve.

44:08.620 --> 44:09.360
They call it H.

44:09.720 --> 44:10.540
Hypothesis H.

44:10.740 --> 44:11.980
The proposition that

44:11.980 --> 44:13.240
publishing the two essays

44:13.240 --> 44:14.420
in the public repository

44:14.420 --> 44:15.520
in their present,

44:15.780 --> 44:16.760
untested state

44:16.760 --> 44:17.560
is warranted.

44:17.560 --> 44:18.900
And to mathematically

44:18.900 --> 44:20.260
prove or disprove H,

44:20.400 --> 44:21.340
they develop an intricate

44:21.340 --> 44:22.240
logical system.

44:22.560 --> 44:23.500
They use a dependent

44:23.500 --> 44:24.300
type theory

44:24.300 --> 44:25.580
in the style of the calculus

44:25.580 --> 44:26.920
of inductive constructions

44:26.920 --> 44:28.460
and they combine it

44:28.460 --> 44:29.440
with a bespoke process

44:29.440 --> 44:30.120
algebra called

44:30.120 --> 44:30.660
sphere pop.

44:30.660 --> 44:32.700
Okay, we need to slow down

44:32.700 --> 44:33.700
and unpack these terms

44:33.700 --> 44:34.660
because this is where

44:34.660 --> 44:35.320
the math gets

44:35.320 --> 44:37.180
intensely abstract.

44:38.280 --> 44:39.140
Let's start with

44:39.140 --> 44:40.220
dependent type theory.

44:40.700 --> 44:41.720
What does that actually mean

44:41.720 --> 44:42.440
in the context

44:42.440 --> 44:43.880
of making a decision?

44:43.880 --> 44:45.360
Think of it this way.

44:45.620 --> 44:47.140
In standard computer programming,

44:47.360 --> 44:48.200
you have data types.

44:48.400 --> 44:49.340
A number is a type.

44:49.440 --> 44:50.500
A line of text is a type.

44:51.140 --> 44:52.400
In dependent type theory,

44:52.540 --> 44:54.360
the types can depend on values.

44:54.660 --> 44:55.480
Okay, so?

44:55.900 --> 44:57.120
This allows the programming

44:57.120 --> 44:58.220
language itself

44:58.220 --> 44:59.580
to act as a mathematical

44:59.580 --> 45:00.420
proof checker.

45:00.680 --> 45:01.760
You aren't just writing code

45:01.760 --> 45:02.740
that runs a calculation.

45:03.260 --> 45:04.360
The structure of the code

45:04.360 --> 45:05.700
is the proof.

45:06.280 --> 45:07.220
If the code compiles

45:07.220 --> 45:08.560
successfully without errors,

45:08.800 --> 45:09.940
the mathematical proposition

45:09.940 --> 45:11.280
is proven to be true.

45:12.240 --> 45:13.420
Flyxion is trying to write

45:13.420 --> 45:14.320
a computer program

45:14.320 --> 45:15.220
where the successful

45:15.220 --> 45:16.420
compilation of the code

45:16.420 --> 45:18.100
acts as a mathematical guarantee

45:18.100 --> 45:19.660
that publishing the essays

45:19.660 --> 45:20.800
is the right choice.

45:20.960 --> 45:22.020
That is wild.

45:23.000 --> 45:24.000
And what about sphere pop?

45:24.140 --> 45:24.820
It sounds like

45:24.820 --> 45:26.620
a brand of bubble gum

45:26.620 --> 45:27.140
or candy.

45:27.780 --> 45:29.180
What is a process algebra

45:29.180 --> 45:30.920
and how does sphere pop work?

45:30.920 --> 45:32.600
Process algebra is a way

45:32.600 --> 45:34.100
to mathematically model

45:34.100 --> 45:35.100
concurrent systems.

45:35.500 --> 45:36.600
Systems where multiple things

45:36.600 --> 45:37.100
are happening

45:37.100 --> 45:37.940
and interacting

45:37.940 --> 45:38.740
at the same time.

45:39.320 --> 45:41.120
Flyxion invented sphere pop

45:41.120 --> 45:42.420
to model the life cycle

45:42.420 --> 45:43.060
of the decision.

45:43.060 --> 45:44.240
Okay, I'm tracking.

45:44.820 --> 45:45.880
Imagine computation

45:45.880 --> 45:46.980
proceeding by popping

45:46.980 --> 45:48.040
nested circles.

45:48.800 --> 45:49.440
Think of a circle

45:49.440 --> 45:50.460
drawn on a piece of paper.

45:50.940 --> 45:51.660
Inside that circle

45:51.660 --> 45:53.060
is a mathematical operator

45:53.060 --> 45:54.700
and a list of arguments

45:54.700 --> 45:55.340
or conditions.

45:55.840 --> 45:56.920
When all the conditions

45:56.920 --> 45:57.740
inside the circle

45:57.740 --> 45:58.220
are met

45:58.220 --> 45:59.640
and become stable values,

46:00.000 --> 46:00.700
the circle pops.

46:00.880 --> 46:01.740
Oh, like popping a bubble.

46:02.340 --> 46:02.800
Exactly.

46:03.300 --> 46:04.540
It evaluates the operator

46:04.540 --> 46:06.200
and replaces the entire circle

46:06.200 --> 46:07.100
with the final result.

46:07.440 --> 46:08.420
The system evaluates

46:08.420 --> 46:09.320
a complex decision

46:09.320 --> 46:10.300
from the inside out,

46:10.660 --> 46:11.680
popping nested circles

46:11.680 --> 46:12.140
of logic

46:12.140 --> 46:13.020
until you are left

46:13.020 --> 46:13.960
with a single answer.

46:14.500 --> 46:16.380
So, Flyxion uses this

46:16.380 --> 46:17.500
to model the life cycle

46:17.500 --> 46:18.240
of an idea

46:18.240 --> 46:19.800
moving through states

46:19.800 --> 46:20.300
of commitment,

46:20.660 --> 46:21.600
like open,

46:22.000 --> 46:22.400
bound,

46:23.140 --> 46:23.700
transformed,

46:24.780 --> 46:25.180
verified,

46:25.600 --> 46:26.440
and finally

46:26.440 --> 46:28.300
collapsed into a decision.

46:28.820 --> 46:29.160
Yes.

46:29.700 --> 46:30.020
So,

46:30.160 --> 46:30.740
they are trying

46:30.740 --> 46:32.200
to mathematically model

46:32.200 --> 46:33.540
the psychological act

46:33.540 --> 46:34.040
of deciding

46:34.040 --> 46:34.900
to hit publish.

46:34.900 --> 46:36.160
And to do this,

46:36.660 --> 46:37.480
they forced the math

46:37.480 --> 46:38.020
to evaluate

46:38.020 --> 46:39.300
five distinct options.

46:39.800 --> 46:40.460
Let's walk through

46:40.460 --> 46:40.880
the choices

46:40.880 --> 46:41.940
they fed into the system.

46:42.600 --> 46:43.220
Option one.

46:43.580 --> 46:44.160
Publish now.

46:44.680 --> 46:45.940
Just put the raw text

46:45.940 --> 46:46.420
out there

46:46.420 --> 46:47.820
and let the internet

46:47.820 --> 46:48.400
deal with it.

46:48.780 --> 46:49.440
Option two.

46:49.740 --> 46:50.640
Publish with status.

46:51.500 --> 46:52.460
This means they publish

46:52.460 --> 46:53.000
the text,

46:53.120 --> 46:53.680
but they attach

46:53.680 --> 46:55.220
specific editorial caveats.

46:55.640 --> 46:56.420
A giant disclaimer

46:56.420 --> 46:56.940
that says,

46:57.360 --> 46:57.580
warning,

46:58.120 --> 46:59.100
this code is untested.

47:00.240 --> 47:01.080
Option three.

47:01.280 --> 47:01.940
Option three.

47:02.640 --> 47:03.420
Hold with hash.

47:03.420 --> 47:05.000
This is fascinating.

47:05.160 --> 47:06.300
It means they don't

47:06.300 --> 47:07.220
publish the text,

47:07.360 --> 47:08.120
but they generate

47:08.120 --> 47:09.860
a cryptographic hash

47:09.860 --> 47:10.620
of the essays

47:10.620 --> 47:11.480
and publish that.

47:11.800 --> 47:12.760
It proves to the world

47:12.760 --> 47:13.740
they had the idea

47:13.740 --> 47:14.820
on a specific date

47:14.820 --> 47:15.980
without actually

47:15.980 --> 47:17.440
revealing the idea itself.

47:17.960 --> 47:18.640
Option four.

47:19.280 --> 47:20.240
Hold without hash.

47:21.060 --> 47:21.760
Total secrecy.

47:22.060 --> 47:22.700
Keep it in a drawer.

47:23.400 --> 47:24.460
And option five.

47:24.920 --> 47:25.920
Publish with status

47:25.920 --> 47:26.880
and run.

47:27.400 --> 47:27.720
Meaning,

47:27.980 --> 47:28.740
wait until they actually

47:28.740 --> 47:29.180
have the time

47:29.180 --> 47:29.920
to run the code,

47:30.400 --> 47:31.300
verify the results,

47:31.460 --> 47:32.120
and then publish

47:32.120 --> 47:32.860
the whole package.

47:33.420 --> 47:34.460
To evaluate

47:34.460 --> 47:35.440
these five options,

47:35.600 --> 47:36.520
the mathematical model

47:36.520 --> 47:37.240
grades them against

47:37.240 --> 47:38.580
four specific attributes

47:38.580 --> 47:39.260
or risks.

47:39.980 --> 47:41.000
First is record.

47:41.400 --> 47:42.540
How well does the option

47:42.540 --> 47:43.720
prove the author's priority

47:43.720 --> 47:44.680
in having the idea?

47:45.140 --> 47:46.000
Second is feedback.

47:46.600 --> 47:47.580
How much valuable insight

47:47.580 --> 47:48.380
can the author gain

47:48.380 --> 47:49.700
from others reading the work?

47:50.160 --> 47:51.660
Third is misreading exposure.

47:52.200 --> 47:53.120
The severe risk

47:53.120 --> 47:53.560
of the public

47:53.560 --> 47:54.620
ignoring the nuance

47:54.620 --> 47:55.580
and assuming the essays

47:55.580 --> 47:56.240
are a proven,

47:56.540 --> 47:57.240
finished product

47:57.240 --> 47:58.160
rather than

47:58.160 --> 47:59.320
a theoretical design.

47:59.800 --> 48:00.760
And fourth is

48:00.760 --> 48:01.620
revision exposure.

48:01.620 --> 48:02.620
The personal

48:02.620 --> 48:03.540
and professional risk

48:03.540 --> 48:04.880
of having to publicly

48:04.880 --> 48:06.020
retract the essays

48:06.020 --> 48:07.640
if the untested code

48:07.640 --> 48:08.480
ultimately fails?

48:09.260 --> 48:09.860
So they feed

48:09.860 --> 48:10.720
all these options

48:10.720 --> 48:11.640
and all these risks

48:11.640 --> 48:12.960
into the dependent type

48:12.960 --> 48:14.000
theory and the popping

48:14.000 --> 48:14.980
circles of sphere pop.

48:15.400 --> 48:16.600
And what does

48:16.600 --> 48:17.280
the math say?

48:17.860 --> 48:19.040
Does the supercomputer

48:19.040 --> 48:19.980
give them an answer?

48:20.520 --> 48:21.520
The system evaluates

48:21.520 --> 48:22.860
the choices using a concept

48:22.860 --> 48:24.060
called Pareto dominance.

48:24.740 --> 48:25.360
In economics,

48:25.520 --> 48:26.040
one option

48:26.040 --> 48:26.900
Pareto dominates

48:26.900 --> 48:27.860
another if it is

48:27.860 --> 48:28.860
better in at least

48:28.860 --> 48:29.420
one regard

48:29.420 --> 48:30.220
and strictly

48:30.220 --> 48:31.100
no worse

48:31.100 --> 48:31.980
than any other regard.

48:32.300 --> 48:32.400
Right.

48:32.660 --> 48:33.520
The math successfully

48:33.520 --> 48:34.240
proves that certain

48:34.240 --> 48:35.480
options dominate others.

48:36.080 --> 48:36.560
For instance,

48:36.840 --> 48:38.300
publishing with strict caveats

48:38.300 --> 48:39.660
obviously dominates

48:39.660 --> 48:40.940
publishing without caveats

48:40.940 --> 48:42.120
because it reduces

48:42.120 --> 48:43.120
misreading exposure

48:43.120 --> 48:43.840
without hurting

48:43.840 --> 48:44.660
the feedback score.

48:44.780 --> 48:45.220
Makes sense.

48:45.920 --> 48:46.640
But when the system

48:46.640 --> 48:47.280
tries to choose

48:47.280 --> 48:48.160
between publish

48:48.160 --> 48:48.940
with caveats

48:48.940 --> 48:49.720
and hold

48:49.720 --> 48:50.940
with cryptographic hash,

48:51.360 --> 48:52.060
the mathematics

48:52.060 --> 48:52.840
gridlock.

48:53.360 --> 48:54.160
It gets stuck.

48:54.920 --> 48:55.500
Choosing between

48:55.500 --> 48:56.220
those two options

48:56.220 --> 48:57.300
requires a fundamental

48:57.300 --> 48:58.800
subjective value judgment.

48:59.440 --> 49:00.380
Do you personally

49:00.380 --> 49:01.620
value public feedback

49:01.620 --> 49:02.900
more than you fear

49:02.900 --> 49:03.580
public revision?

49:04.360 --> 49:05.240
The formal mathematical

49:05.240 --> 49:06.200
logic explicitly

49:06.200 --> 49:06.940
halts and says,

49:07.340 --> 49:08.120
I cannot make this

49:08.120 --> 49:09.200
value judgment for you.

49:09.620 --> 49:10.240
It's incredible.

49:10.660 --> 49:11.740
So what does this

49:11.740 --> 49:12.200
all mean?

49:12.960 --> 49:13.820
It's like trying to use

49:13.820 --> 49:14.820
a multi-million dollar

49:14.820 --> 49:15.420
supercomputer

49:15.420 --> 49:16.940
to decide what you

49:16.940 --> 49:17.820
should have for lunch,

49:18.240 --> 49:19.040
only for the

49:19.040 --> 49:20.200
supercomputer to churn

49:20.200 --> 49:20.980
for three days

49:20.980 --> 49:21.900
and finally print out

49:21.900 --> 49:22.780
a receipt that says,

49:22.980 --> 49:24.500
I cannot taste food.

49:25.180 --> 49:25.960
That's a brilliant

49:25.960 --> 49:26.480
analogy.

49:26.480 --> 49:27.940
All of this towering

49:27.940 --> 49:29.080
incredible mathematical

49:29.080 --> 49:30.720
architecture just to

49:30.720 --> 49:31.680
mathematically prove

49:31.680 --> 49:33.060
that math cannot make

49:33.060 --> 49:34.540
a human value judgment.

49:35.280 --> 49:36.380
It perfectly illustrates

49:36.380 --> 49:37.200
the boundary of

49:37.200 --> 49:37.840
formal logic.

49:38.780 --> 49:39.260
And Flyxion

49:39.260 --> 49:40.040
doesn't stop there.

49:40.400 --> 49:41.160
They also try to

49:41.160 --> 49:42.180
incorporate external

49:42.180 --> 49:43.800
highly unlikely risks

49:43.800 --> 49:44.600
into this calculus.

49:45.220 --> 49:46.000
They introduce an

49:46.000 --> 49:46.760
equation they call

49:46.760 --> 49:48.440
the semi-paranoid

49:48.440 --> 49:49.180
risk rule.

49:49.580 --> 49:51.160
Yes, the semi-paranoid

49:51.160 --> 49:51.480
rule.

49:52.240 --> 49:53.340
This section is

49:53.340 --> 49:54.400
brilliant because it

49:54.400 --> 49:55.120
deals with the

49:55.120 --> 49:56.520
anxieties we all have

49:56.520 --> 49:57.580
about the digital world.

49:58.300 --> 49:59.360
Flyxion was deeply

49:59.360 --> 50:00.040
worried about what

50:00.040 --> 50:01.500
would happen if the

50:01.500 --> 50:02.740
massive tech provider

50:02.740 --> 50:03.540
hosting their code

50:03.540 --> 50:04.860
repositories suddenly

50:04.860 --> 50:06.140
arbitrarily deleted

50:06.140 --> 50:06.780
their accounts.

50:06.900 --> 50:07.460
Which happens.

50:07.640 --> 50:08.560
It happens to people

50:08.560 --> 50:09.460
all the time.

50:09.880 --> 50:11.100
But Flyxion had no

50:11.100 --> 50:11.800
way of knowing the

50:11.800 --> 50:13.340
actual probability of

50:13.340 --> 50:14.280
that happening to them.

50:14.860 --> 50:16.240
So how do you

50:16.240 --> 50:17.320
mathematically decide

50:17.320 --> 50:18.220
whether to spend time

50:18.220 --> 50:18.960
and money protecting

50:18.960 --> 50:19.960
against a disastrous

50:19.960 --> 50:21.420
event when you have

50:21.420 --> 50:23.000
absolutely no idea

50:23.000 --> 50:23.720
what the odds are?

50:24.500 --> 50:25.400
The author constructs

50:25.400 --> 50:27.140
a mini-max-regret rule.

50:27.620 --> 50:28.380
The equation they

50:28.380 --> 50:29.000
arrive at is

50:29.000 --> 50:30.060
surprisingly elegant.

50:30.860 --> 50:31.600
You should mitigate

50:31.600 --> 50:32.820
the risk if,

50:33.320 --> 50:34.280
and only if,

50:34.680 --> 50:35.980
two times the cost

50:35.980 --> 50:36.740
of the mitigation

50:36.740 --> 50:38.380
is less than the

50:38.380 --> 50:39.380
total loss

50:39.380 --> 50:40.580
minus the residual

50:40.580 --> 50:41.100
loss.

50:41.340 --> 50:41.620
Okay.

50:42.020 --> 50:43.140
2K is less than

50:43.140 --> 50:43.880
L minus P.

50:44.220 --> 50:44.540
Right.

50:45.240 --> 50:46.140
Flyxion calls it

50:46.140 --> 50:47.420
semi-paranoid for a

50:47.420 --> 50:48.620
very specific reason.

50:49.280 --> 50:50.460
It is paranoid enough

50:50.460 --> 50:51.460
to protect you against

50:51.460 --> 50:52.800
catastrophic risks,

50:52.980 --> 50:53.620
even when the

50:53.620 --> 50:54.800
probability is totally

50:54.800 --> 50:55.240
unknown.

50:55.900 --> 50:57.300
But it's only semi-paranoid

50:57.300 --> 50:58.780
because the 2K multiplier

50:58.780 --> 51:00.360
actively bounds your fear.

51:00.560 --> 51:01.300
How does it do that?

51:01.620 --> 51:02.520
It refuses any

51:02.520 --> 51:03.460
mitigation strategy

51:03.460 --> 51:04.520
that costs as much

51:04.520 --> 51:05.640
as half the loss

51:05.640 --> 51:06.200
it avoids.

51:06.660 --> 51:07.340
It mathematically

51:07.340 --> 51:08.500
bounds the paranoia

51:08.500 --> 51:09.200
so you don't spend

51:09.200 --> 51:09.900
your entire life

51:09.900 --> 51:11.140
building digital bunkers.

51:11.280 --> 51:12.160
It's a perfect algorithm

51:12.160 --> 51:12.980
for managing

51:12.980 --> 51:14.380
modern digital anxiety.

51:15.020 --> 51:15.680
And speaking of

51:15.680 --> 51:16.560
digital anxieties,

51:17.120 --> 51:17.860
they also tackle

51:17.860 --> 51:18.840
the illusion of

51:18.840 --> 51:20.260
the contribution graph.

51:20.260 --> 51:22.060
Oh, this is so relevant.

51:22.320 --> 51:22.920
Anyone who works

51:22.920 --> 51:23.820
in software development

51:23.820 --> 51:24.660
knows about the

51:24.660 --> 51:26.140
GitHub contribution graph.

51:26.820 --> 51:27.240
It's those little

51:27.240 --> 51:28.240
green squares on your

51:28.240 --> 51:29.020
profile that show

51:29.020 --> 51:29.920
how many code commits

51:29.920 --> 51:30.820
you make every single day.

51:31.100 --> 51:32.180
It has become a

51:32.180 --> 51:33.400
massive proxy metric

51:33.400 --> 51:34.240
for how hard

51:34.240 --> 51:35.160
a programmer works.

51:35.860 --> 51:36.380
But Flyxion

51:36.380 --> 51:37.300
mathematically proves

51:37.300 --> 51:37.860
that comparing

51:37.860 --> 51:38.660
those green squares

51:38.660 --> 51:39.320
across different

51:39.320 --> 51:39.900
coding cultures

51:39.900 --> 51:41.080
is totally

51:41.080 --> 51:42.280
fundamentally invalid.

51:42.960 --> 51:43.740
They prove it by

51:43.740 --> 51:44.660
focusing on the

51:44.660 --> 51:45.760
